; data-readers.bl — the built-in tagged-literal registry, owned by source.
;
; A tagged literal like `#d[…]` or `#time"…"` reads as a call to a named
; reader function: `#d[…]` → `(datom/read-query […])`, `#time"…"` →
; `(datom.time/read-iso8601 "…")`. The tag→function mapping is DATA, and it
; lives here — not baked into the Elixir reader — exactly as Clojure keeps its
; built-in `#inst` / `#uuid` in a `data_readers.clj` the runtime loads at boot.
;
; Why this file and not a per-library `(data-reader! …)` at the bottom of
; datom.bl / datom.time.bl: the reader reads a WHOLE file into forms before it
; evaluates any of them, so a registration that sits in the same file as the
; first `#tag` literal it needs has already lost the race. `BeamLisp.init/0`
; evaluates THIS file during boot, before any user program is read, so every
; built-in tag is registered in time — the self-hosted analogue of the Elixir
; default that used to hardcode these mappings.
;
; The target functions (datom/read-query, datom.time/read-iso8601) are NOT
; required here: registration only stores the symbol. The symbol resolves when
; a user's `#d` / `#time` literal is actually evaluated, by which point that
; user file has required the owning namespace itself — the same deferral the
; old hardcoded default relied on.
;
; To add a built-in tag: add one `data-reader!` line here. A program may still
; register or shadow a tag at runtime with its own `(data-reader! …)`; the
; registry is last-write-wins, so a later call overrides this seed.

(ns data-readers)

; #d[:find …] — a Datalog query literal, validated at read time.
(data-reader! "d" (quote datom/read-query))

; #time"2026-06-15" / #time"2026/2027" — an ISO 8601-2 (EDTF) temporal value:
; a bounded half-open interval read into a `:db.type/time` value.
(data-reader! "time" (quote datom.time/read-iso8601))
